Title = {A novel nanoparticle-loaded tube embedded phase-change microcapsules
   coatings for temperature regulation performance and fire safety of
   buildings},
Journal = {INTERNATIONAL COMMUNICATIONS IN HEAT AND MASS TRANSFER},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {167},
Number = {A},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {With the increasing demand for comfort in living environments, this
   demand involves both temperature suitability and cleanliness and
   sterility. For the coating of building materials to make them
   temperature appropriate phase change materials (PCMs) are being
   introduced as a heat retention strategy to alleviate energy supply
   constraints and improve building occupancy comfort. Encapsulation of
   PCMs in microcapsules prevents leakage. This study addresses the
   limitations of organic shell materials in PCMs, especially their low
   thermal conductivity and flammability, by incorporating titanium dioxide
   nanotubes (TNTs) with large specific surface area. A novel
   ``dots-tubes-balls{''} structure was prepared. And the peak heat release
   rate (pHRR) was reduced by 41.3 \%. Titanium dioxide nanotubes were
   modified using silver nanoparticles, and the concentration of bacterial
   fluid was reduced by 25.5 \% after 24 h of co-culture. The antimicrobial
   properties of architectural coatings can be improved. These properties
   make these coatings very promising for improving building comfort.},
